WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.759
By the corner shop's door hangs a sign-up sheet: ring me when the fresh bread arrives.

2
00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:11.524
When the bread van comes, the shopkeeper rings every name on it.

3
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:13.801
The van doesn't know who signed up.

4
00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.928
And anyone can cross their name off.

5
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:19.093
The shop's program has the same need.

6
00:00:19.093 --> 00:00:21.537
When an order arrives, three parts care.

7
00:00:21.537 --> 00:00:24.469
The stock view shows what's left on the shelves.

8
00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:27.034
The confirmation email tells the customer.

9
00:00:27.034 --> 00:00:30.883
And the sales chart in the back office counts the day's orders.

10
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.636
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:34.636 --> 00:00:38.705
Why not just call all three from the method that places the order?

12
00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:39.753
It's three lines.

13
00:00:39.753 --> 00:00:41.171
What's wrong with that?

14
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:42.968
Let's look.

15
00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:46.729
place calls the stock view, then the chart, then the email.

16
00:00:46.729 --> 00:00:48.068
Now the chart breaks.

17
00:00:48.068 --> 00:00:51.383
It throws an exception, and place stops right there.

18
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:55.591
The email never goes out, and the order fails, because of a chart.

19
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.543
And a fourth part that wants to hear means editing place again.

20
00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:02.811
The order code knows everyone, and anyone can break it.

21
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:06.714
Wouldn't it be nice if place could just say: an order happened.

22
00:01:06.714 --> 00:01:08.700
And whoever cares, hears about it?

23
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:11.185
That's the sign-up sheet.

24
00:01:11.185 --> 00:01:11.992
Let's map it.

25
00:01:11.992 --> 00:01:16.895
The shopkeeper with the sheet is the subject: the one that knows what happened.

26
00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:19.191
Each name on the sheet is a listener.

27
00:01:19.191 --> 00:01:21.364
Ringing everyone is called publish.

28
00:01:21.364 --> 00:01:24.095
And crossing your name off is unsubscribing.

29
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.787
Here's a small subject that any part of the program can use.

30
00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:34.027
It's called an event bus, because it carries events: values that say what happened.

31
00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:39.014
subscribe adds a listener to the list, and hands back a handle, a way to leave.

32
00:01:39.014 --> 00:01:41.412
In Java a listener is just a Consumer.

33
00:01:41.412 --> 00:01:44.127
In Kotlin, a function that takes the event.

34
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:48.212
publish loops over the list and tells each listener.

35
00:01:48.212 --> 00:01:50.168
Notice the try around each call.

36
00:01:50.168 --> 00:01:54.141
If one listener throws, we write a note, and move on to the next.

37
00:01:55.067 --> 00:02:03.930
The lesson's EventBusDemo subscribes three listeners: the stock view, a sales chart that always throws, and the email sender.

38
00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.419
Ana orders rye bread.

39
00:02:05.419 --> 00:02:06.695
What gets printed?

40
00:02:06.695 --> 00:02:07.829
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.053
The stock view gets Ana's order.

42
00:02:13.053 --> 00:02:16.774
Then the note: a listener failed, the sales chart is down.

43
00:02:16.774 --> 00:02:19.020
And the email sender still gets it.

44
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.824
Now let's break it on purpose.

45
00:02:21.824 --> 00:02:24.533
Delete the try and catch, and run it again.

46
00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:29.273
One line, and then: IllegalStateException, the sales chart is down.

47
00:02:29.273 --> 00:02:31.762
The email sender never hears a thing.

48
00:02:31.762 --> 00:02:34.856
In the real shop, the order itself would fail.

49
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:37.923
So the try isn't there to be polite.

50
00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:43.135
One grumpy chart shouldn't get to hang up the phone on everybody below it on the sheet.

51
00:02:43.135 --> 00:02:45.771
Every listener keeps its failures to itself.

52
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.589
And the handle matters, too.

53
00:02:48.589 --> 00:02:52.233
A listener nobody removes is called a lapsed listener.

54
00:02:52.233 --> 00:03:00.601
The bus holds on to it forever, so the garbage collector, the part of the JVM that frees objects nobody uses, can't free it.

55
00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:03.772
It keeps reacting long after its owner is gone.

56
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.072
Here the sheet picture breaks, twice.

57
00:03:07.072 --> 00:03:12.776
A customer who moves away stops answering, but a listener nobody removed answers forever.

58
00:03:12.776 --> 00:03:17.135
And the shopkeeper rings one number at a time, waiting on each call.

59
00:03:17.135 --> 00:03:22.007
So does publish: every listener runs on the publishing thread, inside place.

60
00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:27.765
So a 300 ms email makes every order 300 ms slower.

61
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:29.457
Now the name.

62
00:03:29.457 --> 00:03:37.300
A subject that keeps a list of listeners, and tells each one what happened without knowing who they are, is the Observer pattern.

63
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:42.468
The rule: give every listener a way out, and never let one listener break the others.

64
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.648
One more thing behind the counter.

65
00:03:45.648 --> 00:03:49.019
Order slips wait on a spike, a metal pin for paper.

66
00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:53.713
Each slip says what to do, and with what: restock rye bread, 12 loaves.

67
00:03:53.713 --> 00:03:57.613
A slip can wait, be done, be crossed out, or be done again.

68
00:03:57.833 --> 00:04:05.541
Turning a request into that kind of slip is the Command pattern: an object that holds what to do, and with which arguments.

69
00:04:05.541 --> 00:04:10.241
Once a request is a value, you can queue it, log it, retry it, and undo it.

70
00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:15.133
The lesson's UndoDemo works on one shelf of rye bread.

71
00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:15.941
Restock 12.

72
00:04:15.941 --> 00:04:16.455
Sell 5.

73
00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:20.863
Each command goes on top of a history, right after it works.

74
00:04:20.863 --> 00:04:21.891
Then, sell 40.

75
00:04:21.891 --> 00:04:25.270
What happens to the shelf, and to the history?

76
00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:26.445
Pause and guess.

77
00:04:29.633 --> 00:04:32.119
Rejected: not enough on the shelf.

78
00:04:32.119 --> 00:04:37.383
Units 7, and the history holds just the sale of 5 and the restock of 12.

79
00:04:37.383 --> 00:04:40.235
One undo takes back the sale: units 12.

80
00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:46.267
Now swap the two lines in run, so the command is recorded before it's applied.

81
00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:49.934
The rejected sale of 40 now sits on top of the history.

82
00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:54.868
The next undo puts back 40 loaves that never left, and the shelf shows 47.

83
00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.157
The baker will want to know where they came from.

84
00:04:58.157 --> 00:05:03.711
So the rule for commands is short: record a command for undo only after it has succeeded.

85
00:05:04.633 --> 00:05:07.516
So, why not just call the three parts directly?

86
00:05:07.516 --> 00:05:11.380
Because then the order knows everyone, and anyone can break it.

87
00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:16.041
The bus tells each listener, each in its own try, with a handle to leave by.

88
00:05:16.041 --> 00:05:19.231
And a slip goes on the history only after it worked.

89
00:05:19.433 --> 00:05:20.405
Quick check.

90
00:05:20.405 --> 00:05:23.646
The email listener takes 300 ms to send.

91
00:05:23.646 --> 00:05:26.643
How much slower does every order get?

92
00:05:29.833 --> 00:05:30.415
300 ms.

93
00:05:30.415 --> 00:05:35.322
Every listener runs on the publishing thread, inside place.

94
00:05:35.322 --> 00:05:37.235
So keep listeners fast.
